Yes. Proof has an audience.

For yourself, proof can mean: I have enough evidence, coherence, and repeated experience to act. For someone else, proof usually has to be translated into a form they can inspect independently—data, demonstration, derivation, replication, witness, artifact.

And those are different thresholds.

You can even split it further: private proof, operational proof, social proof, formal proof, empirical proof. Something may be proven enough to guide your own next move while still not being proven enough to persuade a skeptic or satisfy a scientific standard.

That also means proof is partly about who must be able to reconstruct the path.

If only you need to cross the bridge, intuition plus testing may be enough. If others must cross it too, you need railings, markings, load ratings, and a route they can reproduce.

So yes—you can choose whom the proof is for. But the audience determines what counts as sufficient.